(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In which patients is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome especially dangerous?","choices":["Those with visceral disease","Those with classic Kaposi sarcoma, which is indolent","Those with only oral lesions","Those over seventy years old"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — endemic or visceral disease and this syndrome can be aggressive and rapidly fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which discriminators separate cutaneous T-cell lymphoma from psoriasis, eczema and tinea?","choices":["A positive potassium hydroxide preparation","Response to a two-week course of a potent topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to all of the affected areas of the skin, assessed at review","Chronicity, treatment resistance, large or oddly distributed patches, severe pruritus, follicular hair loss, tumours or erythroderma, nodes, and histology over time","The colour and symmetry of the lesions alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — no single one settles it, which is why longitudinal reassessment is stressed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which systemic agents are first-line in Kaposi sarcoma, and how do they combine with antiretroviral therapy?","choices":["Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy alone is more effective than the combination in advanced disease","Vismodegib and sonidegib; antiretroviral therapy plus a hedgehog inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone","Romidepsin and vorinostat; antiretroviral therapy plus a histone deacetylase inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy plus chemotherapy is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone in advanced disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — combination beats antiretroviral therapy alone once disease is advanced. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A course of field therapy has cleared a patient's actinic keratoses. What must not be assumed?","choices":["That daily sun protection is still required","That a nonhealing lesion should be reviewed promptly","That surveillance can stop, because the surrounding field remains at risk","That the treatment reactions of erythema and crusting were abnormal and should not recur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ongoing surveillance remains necessary despite successful therapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which five features should the pathology report identify in a squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Differentiation grade, Breslow thickness, ulceration, mitotic rate, margin status and lymphovascular invasion","Histopathologic subtype alone, since that dictates treatment selection","Human herpesvirus 8 findings, immune status, and margin status","Differentiation grade, depth or thickness, perineural or perivascular invasion, margin status, and aggressive subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all five, and each of them feeds the decision about Mohs and about follow-up. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What caveat attaches to the 6 mm diameter criterion?","choices":["Larger lesions are never melanoma","The threshold applies only to lesions on the trunk","The threshold applies only to patients over 50","Smaller lesions can be melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the threshold is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which directed procedures are reserved for symptomatic Kaposi sarcoma rather than performed routinely?","choices":["Computed tomography of the chest in every newly diagnosed patient, regardless of respiratory symptoms, findings or stage","No further evaluation once the skin lesions are biopsied","Bronchoscopy for suspected pulmonary disease, and endoscopy driven by symptoms or management rather than routinely in asymptomatic patients","Bronchoscopy and endoscopy in every newly diagnosed patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a chest radiograph is used when pulmonary involvement is possible, but scoping is not routine. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How does the level of invasion (Clark) differ from Breslow thickness, and which is the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["The level of invasion is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and the level is the dominant prognostic variable","The level of invasion is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Breslow is the dominant prognostic variable","The level of invasion is a measured depth; Breslow thickness is the anatomic layer reached, and the level is the dominant prognostic variable","Both describe the same measurement on different scales, and either may be used as the dominant prognostic variable"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Breslow is measured in millimetres and drives margins and staging. Note that the levels are conventionally labelled \"Level I\" to \"Level V\"; Clark is the outside name for the same system. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what are stage 0, stage I and stage II?","choices":["Stage 0 is localised disease thicker than stage I; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to other organs","Stage 0 is melanoma confined to the epidermal region of the skin; stage I is localised disease, only in skin and very thin; stage II is localised disease thicker than stage I","Stage 0 is localised disease only in the skin and very thin; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes","Stage 0 is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is localised disease that is very thin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — 0, I and II are all still local. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the core mechanism of actinic keratosis, and how does it relate to keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic melanocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant but a distinct entity quite separate from keratinocyte carcinoma, with no biologic continuum between them","Human papillomavirus infection of keratinocytes in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is a benign lesion lying on a continuum with verruca rather than with keratinocyte carcinoma","Chronic thermal injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of scarred skin; it is premalignant and lies on a continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma but is unrelated to ultraviolet exposure","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma, not a distinct separate entity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a continuum, not a separate disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which cutaneous T-cell lymphoma features worsen prognosis, and which carries the best?","choices":["Prognosis is uniformly poor regardless of stage","Tumours, erythroderma and lymphadenopathy worsen it; limited patch disease does not reduce survival","Patch and plaque disease worsen it; tumour-stage disease carries the better prognosis","Only lymphadenopathy worsens it; skin findings do not affect prognosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — survival is not reduced in limited patch-stage disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which TNM combination defines stage 0 melanoma?","choices":["Tis, N1a, M0","T0, N0, M1","Tis, N0, M0","T1a, N0, M0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ with no nodal or distant disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which two things must a patient on topical therapy for basal cell carcinoma understand?","choices":["That local inflammation is expected, and that clearance has to be confirmed afterwards","That inflammation means the drug has failed and that treatment should be stopped early","That clearance is assumed once the lesion is no longer visible","That sun protection is unnecessary once treatment begins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the inflammation is not a reason to stop, and clearing visibly is not the same as being clear. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is Kaposi sarcoma confirmed, and what supports the diagnosis on histology?","choices":["Human immunodeficiency virus testing alone, since that establishes the whole context","Biopsy of a representative lesion, with human herpesvirus 8-associated findings supporting it","Biopsy of the largest lesion, with Epstein-Barr virus findings supporting it","Clinical appearance alone, since the violaceous colour is characteristic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the virus links the histology back to the cause. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What must a patient starting antiretroviral therapy for Kaposi sarcoma be told?","choices":["That the lesions may worsen early, as immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","That the lesions will always begin improving within the first two weeks of therapy","That antiretroviral therapy should be stopped if lesions enlarge","That oral lesions never respond to therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — warning them beforehand stops early worsening being read as treatment failure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What triggers a biopsy of an actinic keratosis, and which technique is used?","choices":["Morphology or behaviour raising concern for squamous cell carcinoma, or persistence or recurrence after therapy; shave or punch biopsy","Any actinic keratosis on the face, using an excisional biopsy","Failure of a single cryotherapy treatment, using a deep shave taken so as to sample the whole base of the treated lesion","Any lesion over one centimetre, using a punch biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both triggers and the technique, from the same slide. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are the four possible fates of an individual actinic keratosis?","choices":["It will always progress if left untreated","It may persist, involute, recur, or progress","It may persist, progress, metastasise to nodes, or recur after treatment","It may only persist or progress"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — including involution, which is why individual lesion behaviour is unpredictable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does a biopsy for a suspicious nail lesion require?","choices":["Prompt referral to a dermatologist experienced in nail-unit biopsy, with the biopsy sampling the correct site","A shave biopsy of the nail plate itself, taken in primary care","Observation with serial photographs for three months before any biopsy is taken or referral made","Removal of the entire digit for histologic assessment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the referral is to someone experienced in the nail unit specifically. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which lesion should be selected for skin biopsy in suspected cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["An active, representative, UNTREATED lesion, coordinating with dermatopathology","The oldest and most heavily treated lesion, since it is most established","Any lesion, since the histology is uniform across the disease","A lymph node rather than skin, since staging matters more"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treating a lesion first can obscure the very histology you are trying to read. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How should a suspected basal cell carcinoma be sampled?","choices":["It is a clinical diagnosis and biopsy is not usually required","With a shave or punch biopsy, to confirm the diagnosis","With an excisional biopsy taken to below the lesion, to preserve depth","With a deep shave sampling sufficient depth to separate in situ from invasive disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and the pathology then determines the histologic subtype, which dictates treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Where does the morbidity of basal cell carcinoma come from?","choices":["Perineural spread to the skull base in most cases","Transformation into squamous cell carcinoma","Local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites","Nodal metastasis, which occurs in three to seven per cent of all treated cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — metastatic disease is rare but serious, and hedgehog inhibitor therapy exists for it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What must be assessed when planning basal cell carcinoma treatment?","choices":["Whether it is low-risk versus morpheaform, micronodular, infiltrative, recurrent, or in a tissue-sensitive site","Whether the patient has previously taken oral nicotinamide as chemoprevention for a prior lesion","Whether the lesion is pigmented or not","Whether the patient can tolerate hedgehog inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that assessment directly changes treatment selection. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which initial skin-directed therapies are used in cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids, topical fluorouracil, imiquimod, and photodynamic therapy","Intralesional vincristine, vinblastine, bleomycin, and radiation","Liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel, romidepsin and vorinostat","Topical corticosteroids, topical mechlorethamine, bexarotene gel, and ultraviolet phototherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four skin-directed options. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A single nail abnormality has persisted despite treatment. What must be avoided?","choices":["Removing the nail polish before examination","Repeated empiric antifungal, antibiotic or wart treatment","Photographing the concerning nail before any referral is made","Examining the other nine nails"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — repeating empiric therapy on a solitary persistent nail lesion is how diagnostic delay happens. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Besides sun exposure and immunosuppression, which risk factors apply to squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Red tattoo ink, laser treatment and cryotherapy","Chronic wounds, scars or prior radiation fields; certain genetic diseases; and mucosal or genital disease","Tanning bed use, fair skin that burns easily, male sex, and outdoor occupation without protection","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three additional categories as listed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"In which three immunosuppressed groups is basal cell carcinoma more common and more likely to recur?","choices":["Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, human immunodeficiency virus, and organ transplantation","Diabetes, chronic kidney disease and cirrhosis","Pregnancy, and long-term oral contraceptive or corticosteroid use","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma, solid-organ transplant, and allogeneic stem cell transplant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the three groups named on the risk factor slide. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What regional assessment goes alongside biopsy of a squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Sentinel lymph node biopsy in every case over one centimetre","No regional assessment, since actinically induced disease rarely spreads","Palpate the draining lymph-node basins, considering imaging or nodal evaluation for high-risk tumours","Routine computed tomography of the chest, abdomen and pelvis in every newly diagnosed case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the nodes are examined at diagnosis, not only at follow-up. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which local reactions should an actinic keratosis patient be warned to expect from field therapy?","choices":["Blistering that requires the course to be abandoned","No visible reaction at all if the therapy is working","Erythema and crusting, alongside adherence to the full treatment duration","Permanent hypopigmentation at every treated site, which is expected and harmless"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — patients who are not warned stop the course early. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What harm follows overly aggressive cutaneous T-cell lymphoma therapy?","choices":["Accelerated transformation into a systemic non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Permanent phototherapy intolerance","Loss of eligibility for a cutaneous lymphoma centre","Complications and premature death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which history elements must be elicited?","choices":["Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational or recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; prior skin cancer, immunosuppression or transplant, human immunodeficiency virus risk; chronic scars, wounds or radiation sites; family history","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars, wounds and radiation sites; family history — but not prior skin cancer, immunosuppression, transplant or human immunodeficiency virus status, which the dermatologist obtains at the time of biopsy","Onset and change; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars and radiation sites; family history — but not immunosuppression or transplant status, which do not alter cutaneous malignancy risk","Onset and change, pain or pruritus; occupational and recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; immunosuppression and family history — but not prior skin cancer, which does not predict a second primary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — note chronic scars and radiation sites among them. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"}]};
